{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792356331,"nonce":"zfjbGh9a8crRb0KZHKa+s9sBtzU=","message":"Layers that only fade are in 4d643ea3 (local): the records from a layer's opening to its closing become a nested Layer item, drawn into an exact-size texture and composited with the layer's opacity. Checked by pixels: a red box and a green one over it in a half-opacity group show the green faded over white with no red through it, which per-primitive alpha would not. A layer that blurs, filters its colour, casts a drop shadow or is masked is drawn flat and reported as LayerEffect. Next: those effects. The ABI's blur is an exact Gaussian, Blinc's is a Kawase approximation capped at 8 passes; by tap count the Gaussian is cheaper under about 8px and Kawase above, not measured. Plan: an exact separable Gaussian with CPU-computed weights, and a reduced-resolution blur above roughly 12 to 16px, benchmarked on this machine first. Then backdrop, multi-stop gradients, canvases, local clip, clip-path frame, fade rect, notch.","files":null}]}